## Accounts

Accounts live in the project's PostgreSQL. There is no sign-up page: create them with the CLI,
which reads the password from standard input so that it stays out of the shell's history:

```bash
cd backend
printf '%s\n' "$PASSWORD" | uv run {{ project_slug }} users create --email you@example.com --admin
uv run {{ project_slug }} api-keys create --email you@example.com --name my-script   # printed once
```

{% if with_frontend %}The visor shows a sign-in form until someone is signed in, and `/api/messages`, the example
route, answers only signed-in users. {% endif %}A browser signs in with `POST /api/auth/login` and then holds an
HttpOnly session cookie (7 days without use or 30 in total end it). Scripts send an API key as
`Authorization: Bearer <key>`.
`users set-password`, `users deactivate`, `api-keys list` and `api-keys revoke` do the rest; `--help`
explains each one. Ten wrong passwords in a row pause an account's sign-in for a minute per attempt.
{%- if email == 'smtp' %}
A forgotten password is reset with a one-time code sent by email (`POST /api/auth/password-reset`, then
`/api/auth/password-reset/confirm`); the code works once, for an hour, and resetting ends every session.{% if with_frontend %}
The visor's sign-in form has a "Forgot your password?" button for it.{% endif %}{% endif %}
{%- elif auth == 'oidc' %}

## Sign-in

Accounts live at an OpenID Connect provider (Auth0, Keycloak, Zitadel, Logto, Neon Auth…). Clients
send its tokens as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; the API verifies them against the provider's
published keys and `/api/auth/me` returns the caller{% if with_database %}, kept in the `users` table from the first request{% endif %}.
Set `{{ env_prefix }}AUTH_ISSUER` and `{{ env_prefix }}AUTH_AUDIENCE` in `backend/.env` and on the API's
platform. Use this API's identifier as the audience. Protected routes answer 503 with incomplete settings.
For an issuer dedicated to this application whose session tokens omit `aud`, set
`{{ env_prefix }}AUTH_AUTHORIZED_PARTIES` to the allowed `azp` values instead of an audience.
Clerk uses frontend origins for `azp`. When both settings are present, both claims must match.
{%- if with_frontend %} The visor signs in at
the provider (code flow with PKCE) and sends the token with every request; `/api/messages`, the example route,
answers only signed-in users. It needs `VITE_OIDC_AUTHORITY` and `VITE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID` in `frontend/.env.local` and in
Vercel (`frontend/.env.example`).{% endif %}
{%- endif %}

{% if jobs == 'postgres' %}## Background jobs

Work that may fail or take long runs outside the request, from the `jobs` table in PostgreSQL:
add a task to `backend/src/{{ package_name }}/jobs/tasks.py`, queue it from a route with `queue.enqueue` (in the same
transaction as the change that needs it), and periodic tasks go in its `SCHEDULE`. A failed job is
retried five times with growing delays; `uv run {{ project_slug }} jobs failed` lists the ones that gave up and
`jobs retry --id N` queues one again. {% if api_deploy == 'railway' %}The API process runs the jobs (Railway keeps it running), so nothing
else is needed; a separate worker service with `{{ project_slug }} jobs work` takes long jobs off it.{% elif api_deploy == 'vercel' %}Vercel functions do not run between requests: jobs start right
after the request that queued them, and `.github/workflows/jobs-tick.yml` runs periodic tasks and retries every 10
minutes (on Vercel Pro, a Cron Job on `/api/jobs/tick` can do it every minute).{% else %}The API process runs them while it is awake, and
`.github/workflows/jobs-tick.yml` calls `/api/jobs/tick` every 10 minutes for periodic tasks and retries.{% endif %}
Locally, `make dev` runs them in the API process.

{% endif %}## Deploy

Pushing to `main` deploys; the CI must pass first. These one-time steps connect the services.
None of them is needed to develop locally.
{%- if with_database %}

**Database (Neon).** Create a project at https://console.neon.tech in an EU region (Frankfurt,
`aws-eu-central-1`). Copy two connection strings from "Connect": the **direct** one, and the
**pooled** one (host with `-pooler`).
{%- endif %}

{% if email == 'smtp' %}**Email (SMTP).** Pick a provider that offers SMTP (Resend, Postmark, Amazon SES, Brevo…), verify your
sending domain there, and set on the API's platform `{{ env_prefix }}SMTP_HOST`, `{{ env_prefix }}SMTP_PORT` (587),
`{{ env_prefix }}SMTP_SECURITY` (`starttls`), `{{ env_prefix }}SMTP_USERNAME`, `{{ env_prefix }}SMTP_PASSWORD` and `{{ env_prefix }}EMAIL_FROM`. Check
them from `backend/` with those variables set: `uv run {{ project_slug }} send-test-email --to you@example.com`.

{% endif %}{% if jobs == 'postgres' and api_deploy != 'railway' %}**Background jobs.** Generate a secret (`python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"`) and set it as
`{{ env_prefix }}CRON_SECRET` on the API's platform{% if api_deploy == 'vercel' %} (or `CRON_SECRET`, the name Vercel Cron reads){% endif %} and as the repository secret `CRON_SECRET` in GitHub; add the repository
variable `API_URL` with the address that serves `/api`. `jobs-tick.yml` then runs every 10 minutes.

{% endif %}**Logfire.** Create a project at https://logfire.pydantic.dev. Create a **write token** (Settings →
Write tokens) for the API's `LOGFIRE_TOKEN`, and a **read token** (Settings → Read tokens) for
`{{ project_slug }} obs status`: save it in `~/.config/{{ project_slug }}/logfire-read-token` or export it as `LOGFIRE_READ_TOKEN`.
{%- if with_frontend %} For browser errors,
create a frontend application in the project (Frontend → Applications) and set the two values it generates in Vercel:
its restricted token as `VITE_LOGFIRE_TOKEN` and the URL of the project's region as `VITE_LOGFIRE_BASE_URL`.{% endif %}
{%- if api_deploy == 'render' %}

**API (Render).** Dashboard → New → Blueprint → this repository. Render reads `render.yaml`
(Docker, Frankfurt, free plan, health check `/api/healthz`) and asks for the secrets: {% if with_database %}`DATABASE_URL`
(Neon, direct) and {% endif %}`LOGFIRE_TOKEN`. Its URL will be `https://{{ project_slug }}-api.onrender.com` unless the name
is taken.{% if with_database %} Migrations run at start-up. The free plan sleeps after 15 idle minutes; the first request then takes about a minute.{% endif %}
{%- elif api_deploy == 'railway' %}

**API (Railway).** Install the [Railway CLI](https://docs.railway.com/cli), then from the
repository root: `npm install --prefix .railway`, `railway login`, `railway link` (pick or create
the project), `railway config plan` and `railway config apply`. Then, in the Railway dashboard,
set {% if with_database %}`DATABASE_URL` (Neon, direct; or add a Railway PostgreSQL and reference it) and {% endif %}`LOGFIRE_TOKEN`,
generate a public domain for the service, and turn on "Wait for CI" in its settings.{% if with_database %} Migrations run as the pre-deploy command.{% endif %}
{%- elif api_deploy == 'fastapi_cloud' %}

**API (FastAPI Cloud).** `cd backend && uv run fastapi login`, then `uv run fastapi deploy` once to
create the app (name it `{{ project_slug }}`: its URL becomes `https://{{ project_slug }}.fastapicloud.dev`), then `uv run fastapi cloud setup-ci --secrets-only` to store `FASTAPI_CLOUD_TOKEN` and
`FASTAPI_CLOUD_APP_ID` in GitHub (`.github/workflows/deploy-api.yml` deploys on every green `main`).
Set the secrets: {% if with_database %}`uv run fastapi cloud env set --secret DATABASE_URL "<Neon direct URL>"` and {% endif %}`uv run fastapi cloud env set --secret LOGFIRE_TOKEN "<write token>"`,
plus `uv run fastapi cloud env set {{ env_prefix }}ENVIRONMENT production`.{% if with_database %} Migrations run at start-up.{% endif %}
{%- elif api_deploy == 'vercel' %}

**Visor and API (Vercel).** Import the repository at https://vercel.com/new (root directory: the
repository root; Vercel reads the services from `vercel.json`, region `fra1`). Add the
environment variables {% if with_database %}`DATABASE_URL` (Neon, **pooled**) and {% endif %}`LOGFIRE_TOKEN` for Production and Preview.
{%- if with_database %}
Production deploys come from `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` (migrate, then deploy), not from the
Git integration: run `vercel link` locally and add the repository secrets `DATABASE_URL` (Neon,
**direct**), `VERCEL_TOKEN`, `VERCEL_ORG_ID` and `VERCEL_PROJECT_ID` (the last two are in `.vercel/project.json`).
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if with_frontend and api_deploy != 'vercel' %}

**Visor (Vercel).** Import the repository at https://vercel.com/new with root directory
`frontend`. `frontend/vercel.json` proxies `/api` to `{{ api_public_url }}`: if the API's real URL is
different, change it there. Web Analytics: turn it on in the project (no cookies).

**Origin secret (recommended).** So that nothing reaches the API without going through Vercel,
generate a value (`python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"`) and set it as
`ORIGIN_SECRET` in the Vercel project (Production and Preview: previews call the same API) and as
`{{ env_prefix }}ORIGIN_SECRET` on the API's platform. The API then answers 403 to /api requests that did not
come through the visor's domain (`/api/healthz` excepted), so scripts call `https://<visor>/api/…`.
Without the Vercel variable, Vercel sends the literal text `$ORIGIN_SECRET`: set both or neither.
{%- endif %}

{% if agent == 'pydantic_ai' %}**Agent.** Set the model's key on the API's platform: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for the default model, or
the variable of the provider in `{{ env_prefix }}AI_MODEL`. Until then `/api/agent` answers 503.{% if auth == 'none' %} The route is
public: set a spending limit at the provider.{% endif %} Logfire traces keep the text of questions and
answers{% if auth == 'none' %}, visitors' included{% endif %}; `{{ env_prefix }}AGENT_TRACE_CONTENT=false` keeps only the metadata.

{% endif %}**GitHub.** Protect `main`: require pull requests and the checks `Backend` and `Frontend`, and
allow auto-merge (Settings → General → "Allow auto-merge").

{%- if auth == 'oidc' %}

**Sign-in provider.** Create an application for the API at your OIDC provider (in an EU region if it
offers one) and configure the issuer and audience on the API's platform as described in [Sign-in](#sign-in).
{%- if with_frontend %} Create a single-page application for the visor too: register `http://127.0.0.1:5173`, the production URL and,
if the provider allows patterns, the preview URLs as redirect and logout URLs, and set `VITE_OIDC_AUTHORITY`,
`VITE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID` (and `VITE_OIDC_AUDIENCE` if the provider needs it) in Vercel.{% endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if auth == 'sessions' %}

**First administrator.** Once the API is deployed, create it against the production database:
`printf '%s\n' "$PASSWORD" | DATABASE_URL="<Neon direct URL>" uv run {{ project_slug }} users create --email … --admin` from `backend/`.
{%- endif %}

**After a deploy.** `cd backend && uv run {{ project_slug }} obs status --commit <sha> --wait 15` says when the commit is live
and lists any error since.

## Skills for coding agents

`.agents/skills/` (also `.claude/skills/`) gives agents the project's own procedures and links to
the skills that FastAPI, Typer{% if agent == 'pydantic_ai' %}, Pydantic AI{% endif %} and Logfire ship in their packages, so the guidance matches the
installed versions. More skills that fit this project live in other repositories; add the ones you
want with [`npx skills`](https://github.com/vercel-labs/skills) (it needs Node), which records the
commit of each in `skills-lock.json` (commit it). `--min-release-age=7` runs a release of the tool
that is at least 7 days old, as the rest of the project installs:

```bash
npx --min-release-age=7 skills add pydantic/skills --skill pydantic                # Pydantic models and validation
npx --min-release-age=7 skills add pydantic/skills --skill logfire-query           # query this project's traces
{%- if with_frontend %}
npx --min-release-age=7 skills add pydantic/skills --skill logfire-frontend        # browser errors and Web Vitals
npx --min-release-age=7 skills add microsoft/playwright-cli --skill playwright-cli # drive a browser from the agent
{%- endif %}
{%- if with_database %}
npx --min-release-age=7 skills add neondatabase/agent-skills --skill neon-postgres # the production database
{%- endif %}
{%- if api_deploy == 'render' %}
npx --min-release-age=7 skills add render-oss/skills --skill render-deploy         # deploys on Render
{%- elif api_deploy == 'railway' %}
npx --min-release-age=7 skills add railwayapp/railway-skills --skill use-railway   # deploys on Railway
{%- endif %}
```

They are read as instructions: look at what a skill says before adding it.

## Template updates

This project was generated with [Copier](https://copier.readthedocs.io) from `full-stack-blueprint`.
To take later improvements: commit or stash your work, run `copier update --defaults`, resolve
conflicts, then `make setup && make check`. The template's release notes say what each version
changes (its repository is `_src_path` in `.copier-answers.yml`).
{%- if with_database %}

Switching between `sessions` and database-backed `oidc` requires a data migration after either
sign-in schema has been applied. Before running setup or deploying, restore previously applied
migration files from Git and remove the newly generated, unapplied sign-in bootstrap. Write a
new forward migration that preserves account IDs and references. Rehearse account mapping,
credential enrollment or revocation, and rollback on a restored copy of the database. See
[Switching sign-in modes with existing accounts](https://github.com/ebotiab/full-stack-blueprint/blob/main/docs/choosing-your-options.md#switching-sign-in-modes-with-existing-accounts).
{%- endif %}
